This task is a simplified version of a very common responsibility for DevOps engineers: implementing least-privilege access control in CI/CD systems.

In the real world, you rarely want every developer to have full administrative access to Jenkins. Instead, you grant only the permissions they need to do their job.

For example:

Junior developer (sam): Can view a job, modify its build configuration, and start builds, but cannot delete the job or manage permissions.
Senior developer or release engineer (rohan): May also be allowed to stop running builds, update job metadata, and tag builds, while still not being a Jenkins administrator.

This approach provides several benefits:

Security: Prevents unauthorized changes to critical pipelines.
Compliance: Helps satisfy standards such as ISO 27001, SOC 2, or PCI DSS by enforcing least privilege.
Operational stability: Reduces the risk of accidental deletion or misconfiguration of production pipelines.
Auditability: Makes it clear who is allowed to perform which actions.
